Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• ORDER_ITEMS."user_id": identify the buyer for every purchase line  
• ORDER_ITEMS."created_at": micro‑seconds timestamp when the item (i.e., the purchase) was created – lets us build a purchase timeline for every user  
• ORDER_ITEMS."status": filter to real purchases (e.g., ‘Complete’ or ‘Shipped’) and ignore cancellations / returns  
• ORDERS."order_id": (optional) cross‑check with ORDER_ITEMS; can also be used to reach the order‑level status  
• ORDERS."created_at": additional timestamp at the order level (often the same as, or very close to, the earliest ORDER_ITEMS."created_at" in that order)  
• USERS."id": user dimension table – helps when we want to count all registered users, join demographics, etc. (not strictly required for the retention calculation)  

Typical conditions that may be useful  
• ORDER_ITEMS."status" IN ('Complete','Shipped','Returned')  -- keep only finished sales  
• ORDER_ITEMS."created_at" < 1672531200000000               -- keep records up to 31‑Dec‑2022 23:59:59 (micro‑seconds)  
• WHERE oi_first."created_at" BETWEEN 1577836800000000 AND 1672444800000000  -- users whose first purchase happened from 01‑Jan‑2020 to 31‑Dec‑2022 (example window)  
• For retention windows we will calculate the difference in months between successive purchases and the user’s first purchase.

Below are 10 incremental Snowflake SQL snippets (each with a short annotation) to inspect the data and finally arrive at the requested retention‑percentage result.  
All identifiers are enclosed in double quotes, queries use simple SELECT only, each finishes with LIMIT 20 so you can preview the intermediate outputs.

